#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134663} :activity/id #uuid "7dcd79bf-0e4f-5dab-b7c9-aeea97042511" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049811 :topic/id #uuid "157ebcd0-6389-529a-a695-53136d4482c5" :topic/math-academy-id 2103} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134663 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135045 :tutorial/content "A set of vectors ${U}=\\{ \\mathbf{u}_1, \\cdots, \\mathbf{u}_p \\}$ in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is an **orthogonal set** if each pair of distinct vectors from the set is orthogonal.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following set:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIf we denote the vectors in our set ${W}$ as $\\{ \\mathbf{w}_1, \\mathbf{w}_2, \\mathbf{w}_3 \\},$ we can check that ${W}$ is orthogonal by showing that \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0 \\\\ {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{3}=0 \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's compute each dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=3\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 1=0 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]=3\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot (-4)+1\\cdot 7=0 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]=(-1)\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot (-4)+1\\cdot 7=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo indeed, ${W}$ is an orthogonal set.\n\nIf an orthogonal set is also a basis, then we call it an **orthogonal basis**. Here, $W$ is an orthogonal basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ because it contains three linearly independent (you can check this) orthogonal vectors." :tutorial/id #uuid "dd93c4b2-6ec7-5a9c-a669-7979210393dd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3618 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "86221c16-9568-5f86-9e0e-496ed4975fdb" :step/math-academy-id 20224 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134772}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134664 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133781 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133590 :question/id #uuid "4b092f86-cf53-51b4-9f79-b43d58c8b2e5" :question/math-academy-id "e-5563"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed5c8af7-e617-5e8c-be01-45a26eb17d4d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048849}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134215} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134283} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272106} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272107} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351423} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351542} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371133} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371167}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Sets are Orthonormal"} :step/id #uuid "b9bda0f3-170c-5249-ae0f-9df7c5ca80fa" :step/math-academy-id 20225 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134665}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134665 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133782 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133591 :question/id #uuid "af1ca39e-5cb7-5a93-b88f-0ff86a563bc6" :question/math-academy-id "e-5564"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26f8f84c-bb94-5f3c-a78e-343f9c0c1b1a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048445}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134220} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134280} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272108} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326826} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326836} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351544} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351545} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370894}] :knowledge-point/title "Extending a Set to Form an Orthogonal Basis"} :step/id #uuid "41f3017e-1bb3-5259-a7d8-a79e20fdc3e7" :step/math-academy-id 20226 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134759}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134759 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133837 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133652 :question/id #uuid "7ef88417-f089-51ea-9fac-dccef5825016" :question/math-academy-id "e-7114"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "baf333d2-6bd9-5d79-9c07-85856c81672a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134221} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134518} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272109} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351226} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351227} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351228} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351549} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371119}] :knowledge-point/title "Extending a Set to Form an Orthonormal Basis"} :step/id #uuid "2ccc0684-303e-58b4-854a-e95ea3983445" :step/math-academy-id 26158} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134771 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135090 :tutorial/content "A set of vectors ![](images/96/969c7c332d8285fa0419e716cad301755b3ff1f9f074ab55798ef7c6ea09b8a1.svg) is an **orthonormal set** if it is an orthogonal set of *unit* vectors. Remember that ![](images/25/257a1924bd39970f89e8a9becbac5a5f260572a7e181dedc91ebcc4bb04b0362.svg) is a unit vector if ![](images/e5/e5ae26b69002c7beb7ee09911c2f5da8f62cd1c4ab687657a7e995d89c3f319a.svg) or equivalently, ![](images/d8/d8b5f1f6c4f3c8955cffeac4e7a4407b0d7a5e26afbf95d87e6dd71cc83c0c47.svg)\n\nAn orthonormal set that is also a basis, like the standard basis, is called an **orthonormal basis**. For instance, the standard basis ![](images/f8/f80114a44b31082d68abe17a7d45f60a6455a147a82001ef548eaed69c0c609d.svg) of ![](images/46/4607eb52aa0195587414ef27616970a6b47226a3bb476e944ac079422dc1e1b0.svg) is an orthonormal basis because the vectors are orthogonal to each other and each vector in the set is a unit vector.\n\nThere are two more things to note about orthogonal sets:\n\n- Any orthogonal set of non-zero vectors is linearly independent.\n\n- An orthogonal set in ![](images/5b/5b243567f6e2c78e286689189eb1336948b7e67d92e7c15472ca77e9fcff3bb0.svg) cannot contain more than ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) vectors. More precisely, if ![](images/59/5927da2243d5e3815e5201f00b72ebe3eae6119ba048eaf7a0505c5e1fddffbc.svg) is an orthogonal set of non-zero vectors and ![](images/6b/6b4f5fffc4d51993128d1ad6ebdbf0c543dad75a0295656991f927ed23ae3714.svg) then we must have that ![](images/15/15cac3b851d0f5feb26cd7d31c10fc21eac370c2addcdbd8634030f85bc3323b.svg)\n\n**Note:** To remember that an orthogonal set of non-zero vectors in ![](images/5b/5b243567f6e2c78e286689189eb1336948b7e67d92e7c15472ca77e9fcff3bb0.svg) cannot contain more than ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) vectors, you can use the following intuitive mnemonic. There are only ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) dimensions in ![](images/55/552bf86a483b6d2163ddc2f4ed2748c55cb24e34b4687567631f9a92db8bc158.svg) and each non-zero orthogonal vector has a ![](images/95/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg)-dimensional span, meaning that it \"takes up\" ![](images/95/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg) dimension of ![](images/9c/9cc82a4b9e5c3344d1b66c539046dd27eeb1a53aadd2beba64562c3b4053e84e.svg) So, you can't \"fit\" more than ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) orthogonal vectors in ![](images/9c/9cc82a4b9e5c3344d1b66c539046dd27eeb1a53aadd2beba64562c3b4053e84e.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "85873149-e58a-571e-8f5f-08cb9460ee47" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4353 :tutorial/title "Orthonormal Sets and Orthonormal Bases"} :step/id #uuid "e07126c3-5fe7-512e-b359-08bb40e1e823" :step/math-academy-id 26466 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134664}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134772 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133843 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133658 :question/id #uuid "8a41b0e9-c029-5175-80c9-5d9c6c1dd5f0" :question/math-academy-id "e-7257"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee1838df-f1d6-5bc4-908c-b96e2ad577c9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049951}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134222} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134286} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272104} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326761} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351421} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351422} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351535}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Sets are Orthogonal"} :step/id #uuid "a1b3d3e8-2043-5c48-b762-c8181abbe670" :step/math-academy-id 26850 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134771}}] :activity/title "Orthogonal Sets in Euclidean Spaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133990}]}
